\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{A Hurwitz basis and its norm-one units}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{In the rational Hamilton quaternion algebra, write
#{\qi^2=\qj^2=-1} and #{\qk=\qi\qj=-\qj\qi}.
The \vocabk{Hurwitz order}{fgap-0016} has ordered
#{\mathbb Z}-basis
##{(1,\qi,\qj,h),\qquad h=(1+\qi +\qj +\qk)/2.}
The earlier generator is #{\omega=h-1}.
This lattice is a ring stable under quaternion conjugation.
For #{x=a+b\qi +c\qj +dh}, its norm is
##{N(x)=a^2+b^2+c^2+d(a+b+c)+d^2\in\mathbb Z.}
Its units are exactly the #{24} norm-one elements
##{\{\pm 1,\pm\qi,\pm\qj,\pm\qk\}
 \ \cup\ 
 \{(\pm 1\pm\qi\pm\qj\pm\qk)/2\}.}
They form the Hurwitz-unit realization of #{2T} in
\ref{fdip-007G}.}

\proof{
\p{Changing from #{(1,\qi,\qj,\omega)} to the displayed
basis adds the first basis vector to the last, so is integral
and invertible. The nontrivial products of its generators are
##{\begin{aligned}
 \qi\qj&=2h-1-\qi -\qj,&
 \qj\qi&=-2h+1+\qi +\qj,\\
 \qi h&=h-1-\qj,& h\qi&=\qi +\qj -h,\\
 \qj h&=\qi +\qj -h,& h\qj&=h-1-\qi,\\
 h^2&=h-1.
\end{aligned}}
Together with #{\qi^2=\qj^2=-1}, these prove closure.
Conjugation negates #{\qi,\qj} and sends #{h} to #{1-h},
so it preserves the lattice.}

\p{The 4 rational coordinates of #{x} in the basis
#{(1,\qi,\qj,\qk)} are
#{(a+d/2,b+d/2,c+d/2,d/2)}. Their squared sum is the
displayed norm. Quaternion multiplication and reversal of
products under conjugation give #{N(xy)=N(x)N(y)}.
The norm of a nonzero rational quaternion is positive.
If #{x} is an integral unit, its norm and its inverse's norm
are positive integers with product one; hence #{N(x)=1}.
Conversely #{N(x)=1} gives #{x^{-1}=\bar x} in the lattice.}

\p{The 4 coordinates are either all integers or all
half-integers. Integer coordinates with squared sum one have
one entry #{\pm 1} and the other entries zero, giving #{8}
units. Half-integer coordinates with squared sum one all
have absolute value #{1/2}, giving #{16} units. They are the
listed elements. The quaternion action and tetrahedral
identification are those of \ref{fdip-007G} and
\ref{fdip-00M6}.}
}
